        UBERON:0035652 .

TARA:1840369 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Yuan-Primary Point" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    dcterms:description "Each of the twelve main meridians has a Yuan-Primary Point, which is located on the limbs. The Chinese character (Yuan) means primary qi in this context. The chapter \"The 66th Medical Problem\" in Classic of Medical Problems describes the relation between the Yuan-Primary Points and Yuan-Primary Qi. The Yuan-Primary Qi, originating below the umbilicus and between the kidneys, is dispersed to the zang-fu organs and further to the limbs via Triple Energizer. The sites where the Yuan-Primary Qi is retained are Yuan-Primary Points, which are used to treat disorders of the zang-fu organs. In the yin meridians, the Yuan-Primary Poims overlap with the Shu-Stream Points of the Five Shu Points. Each yang meridian, however, has its Yuan-Primary Point other than the Shu-Stream Point." ;
    tara-ap:hasSpecialPointRole TARA:3455429 ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
            owl:intersectionOf ( TARA:4943316 [ a owl:Restriction ;
                        owl:onProperty tara-op:hasSpecialPointRole ;
                        owl:someValuesFrom TARA:3455429 ] ) ] .

TARA:2954517 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Jing-Well Point" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    dcterms:description "One of the five Shu Points, the Jing-Well point is situated in the place where the meridian qi starts to bubble." ;
    tara-ap:hasSpecialPointRole TARA:3839815 ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
            owl:intersectionOf ( TARA:1787498 [ a owl:Restriction ;
                        owl:onProperty tara-op:hasSpecialPointRole ;
                        owl:someValuesFrom TARA:3839815 ] ) ] .

TARA:3861175 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Back-Shu Point" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    dcterms:description "Back-Shu points are specific points on the back where the qi of the respective zang-fu organs is infused. It is stated in Chapter 51 of Miraculous Pivot that \"in the Back-Shu Points you are looking for the reactionary spots of tenderness and soreness, or the points on which pressure exerted relieves pain and discomforts of the patient\". Situated close to their respectively related zang-fu organs, the Back-Shu points present abnormal reactions to the dysfunction of their corresponding zang-fu organs. They are often used for disorders of the internal organs." ;
    tara-ap:hasSpecialPointRole TARA:9507527 ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
            owl:intersectionOf ( TARA:2614885 [ a owl:Restriction ;
                        owl:onProperty tara-op:hasSpecialPointRole ;
                        owl:someValuesFrom TARA:9507527 ] ) ] .

TARA:6200120 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Main Acupuncture Meridian"@en ;
    IAO:0000115 "One of the twelve Meridians that is directly affiliated with a zang or fu organ system via hasAssociatedOrgan, and that is linked to the other eleven principal meridians in a fixed circulatory sequence."@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 5, Sec. II, The Twelve Main Meridians, Page 65. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7."@en ;
    dcterms:description """As the major part in the meridian system, the twelve main meridians share the following features. Each with its own acupuncture points is distributed on a fixed portion of the body surface; each pertains to either a zang or a fu organ (those that pertain to the zang organ communicating with the fu organ, and vice versa). 

The twelve main meridians link one another in a fixed order. A cyclical now of qi is maintained by the connection of the meridians of the hand and foot, yin and yang, exterior and interior."""@en ;
    tara-ap:hasSynonym "Main Acupuncture Channel",
        "Main Meridian",
        "Primary Acupuncture Channel",
        "Primary Meridian" ;
    rdfs:subClassOf TARA:7226516 .

TARA:6705086 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Shu-Stream Point" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    dcterms:description "One of the five Shu Points, the Shu-Stream point is where the meridian qi flourishes. In the yin meridians, the Yuan-Primary Points overlap with the Shu-Stream points of the five Shu Points. Each yang meridian, however, has its Yuan-Primary Point other than the Shu-Stream Point." ;
    tara-ap:hasSpecialPointRole TARA:1716565 ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
            owl:intersectionOf ( TARA:1787498 [ a owl:Restriction ;
                        owl:onProperty tara-op:hasSpecialPointRole ;
                        owl:someValuesFrom TARA:1716565 ] ) ] .

TARA:7315457 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Jing-River Point" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    dcterms:description "One of the five Shu Points, the Jing-River point is where the meridian qi is pouring abundantly." ;
    tara-ap:hasSpecialPointRole TARA:1758929 ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
            owl:intersectionOf ( TARA:1787498 [ a owl:Restriction ;
                        owl:onProperty tara-op:hasSpecialPointRole ;
                        owl:someValuesFrom TARA:1758929 ] ) ] .

TARA:8746709 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Front-Mu Point" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    dcterms:description "Front-Mu points are those points on the chest and abdomen where the qi of the respective zang-fu organs is infused and converged. Located close to their corresponding zang-fu organs, the Front-Mu points play a significant role in the diagnosis and treatment of the disorders of the internal organs." ;
    tara-ap:hasSpecialPointRole TARA:7571236 ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
            owl:intersectionOf ( TARA:2614885 [ a owl:Restriction ;
                        owl:onProperty tara-op:hasSpecialPointRole ;
                        owl:someValuesFrom TARA:7571236 ] ) ] .

TARA:9857829 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Ying-Spring Point" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    dcterms:description "One of the five Shu Points, the Ying-Spring point is where the meridian qi starts to gush." ;
    tara-ap:hasSpecialPointRole TARA:2478120 ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
            owl:intersectionOf ( TARA:1787498 [ a owl:Restriction ;
                        owl:onProperty tara-op:hasSpecialPointRole ;
                        owl:someValuesFrom TARA:2478120 ] ) ] .

tara-ap:hasLocationalDescription a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Locational Description"@en ;
    IAO:0000112 "'LU 1' tara-ap:hasLocationalDescription \"On the anterior thoracic region, at the same level as the first intercostal space, lateral to the infraclavicular fossa, 6 B-cun lateral to the anterior median line.\""@en ;
    dcterms:description "An annotation property that provides the canonical textual description of an acupoint's anatomical location on the body surface. Values follow the WHO Standard Acupuncture Point Locations where available."@en ;
    dcterms:isRequiredBy tara-ap:hasGeneralSurfaceLocation,
        tara-ap:hasRelatedSurfaceAnatomy,
        tara-ap:hasSpecificReferenceLocation,
        tara-op:hasGeneralSurfaceLocation,
        tara-op:hasRelatedSurfaceAnatomy,
        tara-op:hasSpecificReferenceLocation ;
    rdfs:domain TARA:1132428 ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf tara-ap:hasAcupointInformation .

tara-op:hasAssociatedOrgan a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "hasAssociatedOrgan" ;
    IAO:0000112 """Note: Organ affiliation of a meridian is a doctrinal/functional association defined by TCM theory rather than a spatial or mereological relationship.
- 'Lung Meridian' tara-op:hasAssociatedOrgan some 'Lung'."""@en ;
    IAO:0000115 "A Meridian x hasAssociatedOrgan an organ y if and only if traditional Chinese medicine theory designates y as the organ system with which x is affiliated."@en ;
    IAO:0000116 "Not declared as a subproperty of any RO relation, because the organ affiliation of a meridian is a doctrinal/functional association defined by TCM theory rather than a spatial or mereological relationship; located_in, overlaps, and part_of do not accurately describe it, so the property is left ungrounded at the top of the object-property hierarchy. - Fahim Imam"@en ;
    dcterms:description "An object property that relates a meridian to the organ it is associated with according to traditional Chinese medicine theory. Used to express the organ affiliation of a meridian as an OWL axiom."@en ;
    dcterms:isReferencedBy tara-ap:hasAssociatedOrgan ;
    rdfs:range UBERON:0001062 ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf tara-kb:hasMeridianObjectProperty .

<urn:swrl#d> a swrl:Variable .

BFO:0000004 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "independent continuant" ;
    IAO:0000112 "An organism; a heart; a chair; a spatial region." ;
    IAO:0000115 "b is an independent continuant = Def. b is a continuant which is such that there is no c and no t such that b s-depends_on c at t. (axiom label in BFO2 Reference: [017-002])"@en ;
    IAO:0000119 "BFO 2020 (ISO/IEC 21838-2:2021); disjointness with specifically/generically dependent continuant confirmed against the BFO OBO source (BFO_0000004 disjoint_from: BFO_0000020, BFO_0000031)." ;
    IAO:0000412 obo:bfo.owl ;
    rdfs:comment "A continuant that is a bearer of quality and realizable entity entities, in which other entities inhere and which itself cannot inhere in anything."@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf BFO:0000002 ;
    owl:disjointWith BFO:0000020,
        BFO:0000031 .

